<p data-start="1290" data-end="1316">…and so on for every page.</p>
<p data-start="1318" data-end="1516">✅ <strong data-start="1320" data-end="1334">Important:</strong><br data-start="1334" data-end="1337">
The <code data-start="1341" data-end="1346">src</code> path must exactly match the folder name and file name.<br data-start="1401" data-end="1404">
If the image is in <code data-start="1423" data-end="1433">/images/</code> and called <code data-start="1445" data-end="1462">anakin-jedi.jpg</code>, then your path <strong data-start="1479" data-end="1487">must</strong> be <code data-start="1491" data-end="1515">images/anakin-jedi.jpg</code>.</p>
<hr data-start="1518" data-end="1521">
<h2 data-start="1523" data-end="1568">🌍 4. Option 2 — Use <strong data-start="1547" data-end="1568">online image URLs</strong></h2>
<p data-start="1570" data-end="1746">If you don’t want to upload your own pictures, you can use public links — just make sure they start with <code data-start="1675" data-end="1685">https://</code> and point to an actual image file (<code data-start="1721" data-end="1727">.jpg</code>, <code data-start="1729" data-end="1735">.png</code>, <code data-start="1737" data-end="1744">.webp</code>).</p>
