essage-id="763430b1-8756-4b08-b38f-f98a21b436f0" dir="auto" class="min-h-8 text-message relative flex w-full flex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-1" data-message-model-slug="gpt-5"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden first:pt-[1px]"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="181">Perfect — since it’s a <strong data-start="23" data-end="41">YouTube Shorts</strong> video (which is vertical), let’s make it display vertically and fill <strong data-start="111" data-end="137">half the screen height</strong> while keeping it centered and responsive.</p>
<p data-start="183" data-end="259">You can do this entirely with CSS — no need to change your HTML iframe code.</p>
<hr data-start="261" data-end="264">
<h3 data-start="266" data-end="310">✅ Step 1: Keep your iframe HTML the same</h3>
<p data-start="311" data-end="346">Make sure it still looks like this:</p>
